  "body": "<p>This time last week, only one top-five seed remained in the hunt in the Beijing quarterfinals.</p>\n<p>The same is true this week in Wuhan, where World No. 5 Elina Svitolina is the highest-seeded player left in the field.</p>\n<p>Unseeded Zheng Qinwen took advantage of the opportunity in Beijing, and Ann Li, Maria Sakkari, Sonay Kartal and Jelena Ostapenko will look to follow in her footsteps in the final WTA 1000 tournament of the season.</p>\n<p>Here's everything you need to know about the quarterfinal matchups in Wuhan.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n</div><h4>Ann Li vs. [7] Iga Swiatek</h4>\n<h5>Li leads the head-to-head 1-0</h5>\n<p>Li is still riding high after upsetting World No. 2 Aryna Sabalenka, the biggest win of her career. The 26-year-old American showed tremendous poise and resilience in that match, converting her 10th match point in an epic 3-6, 6-4, 7-6 (5) win. </p>\n<p>\"I think on some of those match points I was about to lose my mind,\" she said after the match. \"I was about to crash out.\"</p>\n<p>Instead, she stayed within herself, trusted her shots and persevered. And despite all the whirlwind of emotions that accompanied that match, there was no post-Sabalenka hangover. Li took care of business again on Thursday, beating Janice Tjen in straight sets, and she's into her first WTA 1000 quarterfinal.</p><div class=\"articleWidget center\">\n        <aside class=\"instagram-post embeddable-widget embeddable-widget--floating embeddable-widget--centered-mobile \"><blockquote class=\"instagram-media\" data-instgrm-captioned data-instgrm-permalink=\"https://www.instagram.com/reel/DehPjPYNL8N/?utm_source=ig_embed&amp;utm_campaign=loading\" data-instgrm-version=\"14\" style=\" background:#FFF; border:0; border-radius:3px; box-shadow:0 0 1px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.5),0 1px 10px 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.15); margin: 1px; max-width:540px; min-width:326px; padding:0; width:99.375%; width:-webkit-calc(100% - 2px); width:calc(100% - 2px);\"><div style