ampaign=loading\" style=\" color:#c9c8cd; font-family:Arial,sans-serif; font-size:14px; font-style:normal; font-weight:normal; line-height:17px; text-decoration:none;\" target=\"_blank\">A post shared by WTA (@wta)</a></p></div></blockquote>\n<script async src=\"//www.instagram.com/embed.js\"></script></aside>\n</div><p>She'll face Ostapenko, who has had a far more laborious path to the quarterfinals. She dropped just two games in her first-round win over Daria Kasatkina, but from there she was pushed to her limit by second-seeded Jessica Pegula. After dropping the first set 6-2, she saved three match point in the second-set tiebreak in a thrilling 2-6, 7-6 (7), 6-3 win. She went the distance again on Thursday, beating Leylah Fernandez 6-1, 1-6, 6-4. </p>\n<p>Ostapenko is a former semifinalist in Wuhan, reaching the final four back in 2017.</p>",
